course: reflection
lesson: 31
title: Olympiad: reading hidden feelings in hard times ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In an olympiad puzzle about feelings, the words people say are only one clue; the face, the timing, what they suddenly stop doing and what they call "just" or "only" are clues too, and a good reader puts them together like a detective.
explain: Picture Leo, whose grandad died last month: at school he jokes all day, then snaps at his best friend over a borrowed pencil; one clue alone says "he's fine" or "he's mean", but together they say "he's carrying something heavy".
explain: Two traps hide in the words: hyperbole, where a feeling is blown up ("worst day of my LIFE" after losing a football match), and downplaying, where something big is made small ("it's nothing, just tired" from a friend who has stopped eating lunch); the first usually passes in a day, the second is the one to check.
explain: Feelings can also wear a costume: anger often covers hurt or fear, jokes can cover sadness, and "I don't care" can mean "I care so much it hurts"; grief comes in waves, so a person can laugh in the morning and cry at night, and both are real.
explain: The aim is never to play doctor with your friends, but to notice, ask gently ("You okay? You seem a bit off"), listen, and bring in a trusted adult when the clues point to something big.
fact: Hyperbole is exaggerating on purpose, like "I'm literally dying of boredom"; it shows a strong feeling but is not meant word for word.
fact: Downplaying, or understatement, makes something sound smaller than it is, like "it's just a scratch" about a deep cut; when a real problem is downplayed, it is wise to check and tell an adult.
fact: Anger is often a "cover" feeling: underneath it there may be hurt, fear, sadness or embarrassment.
fact: Grief comes in waves: a grieving person can laugh one moment and cry the next, and both feelings are real.
fact: Numbness, feeling nothing at all, is a common first reaction to a shock or a death, and it does not mean the person didn't care.
fact: A change in someone's usual behaviour, such as a chatty friend going quiet or a friend who stops eating or playing, is often a bigger clue than any single thing they say.
fact: People sometimes say "I don't care" or "whatever" to protect themselves when they actually care a lot.
fact: A grumpy or mean face can come from things you can't see, such as a bad night, worry at home or pain; it is not always about you.
fact: One clue is a guess; several clues pointing the same way make a better guess, but the only way to really know is to ask kindly.
fact: In Britain "not too bad" or "a bit low" can hide a lot, and "mardy" (Midlands and North) means sulky, while "tamping" (Wales) means furious; tone and face help to read them.
fact: Anniversaries, birthdays and holidays can bring grief back strongly, even years later.
fact: If a friend says something that suggests they are being hurt or are in danger, a promise to keep it secret should be broken: tell a trusted adult.
exercise: A month after his grandad died, Leo jokes all day, then snaps at Sam over a pencil. Which reading fits ALL the clues? | Grief leaking out as jokes and anger | He is over it, since he jokes all day | He has always secretly disliked Sam | That pencil really mattered to him a lot
exercise: Mia has a bruise, has stopped eating lunch and says three things: "It's nothing," "I'm just tired," "Don't tell Miss." Which ONE is the strongest sign that an adult should know? | "Don't tell Miss" | "I'm just tired" | "It's nothing" | None of them, she said it's nothing
exercise: Odd one out: three lines are hyperbole and one is downplaying. Which is the downplaying? | "It's just a scratch," with blood on his sock | "I've told you a million times already this week" | "This school bag weighs a ton" | "I nearly died laughing at that"
exercise: Ruby says "worst day of my LIFE" after losing one netball match; Tom says "I'm fine" after a week of not eating lunch. Who needs a more careful check? | Tom: a long change matters more than loud words | Ruby, because she used the biggest words of all | Both the same, because words mean exactly what they say | Neither, because both of them said how they feel
exercise: Grace laughed at a funny story at her nan's funeral tea, then felt awful. What is true? | Grief comes in waves; laughing doesn't mean she didn't love Nan | Laughing proves she has finished grieving and moved on from Nan for good | She shouldn't laugh again for a whole year | Laughing at a funeral tea is always very rude
exercise: When Ollie heard his dog had died, he felt nothing and went to play. His sister said, "You didn't even love him." What is wrong with her reasoning? | Numbness is a common first reaction and doesn't mean he didn't care | Nothing; feeling nothing shows he didn't love the dog | Nothing, because really sad people always cry straight away when they hear | She is wrong only because boys never cry about pets
exercise: Put a good friend's steps in order: (a) listen, (b) notice the change, (c) bring in an adult if it's big, (d) ask gently. | b, d, a, c | d, b, a, c | c, b, d, a | a, c, d, b
exercise: "I don't care if I'm not picked," says Jayden, then hides in the toilets all of break. What does "I don't care" most likely mean? | He cares a lot and is protecting himself | He truly doesn't care at all about the team | He feels ill and needs the school nurse | He wants to be left out again next time
exercise: Miss Patel is short with the class all morning; at lunch you hear she spent the night at the hospital with her son. What does the new clue change? | Her mood came from worry, not from the class | It proves the class behaved very badly | It means she is cross with you in particular | It shows teachers have no feelings at home
exercise: Ella is fine all year but very quiet every 3rd of March. Which reading fits best? | Maybe a sad anniversary | She always has a hard test on that day | She is always ill on that exact date | It is the day she likes the most of all
exercise: After Zak's parents split, four friends talk. ("Mardy" means sulky.) Amir: "He's mardy, ignore him." Bea: "He's been quiet since the split; I'll sit with him and ask." Cole: "He said he's fine, so he is." Dina: "He's quiet, so he did something bad." Who read the clues best? | Bea | Amir | Cole | Dina
exercise: Which pair of clues points most strongly to a friend feeling sad? | Stopped playing football and now eats alone | Wore a red jumper and was late once | Got 8 out of 10 and laughed at a joke | Brought a packed lunch and yawned once
exercise: Gwen from Cardiff says, "I was tamping when she read my diary out." ("Tamping" means furious.) What feeling most likely sat under the anger? | Hurt and embarrassment that her private words were shared | Joy that everyone in the class finally got to hear her lovely writing | Boredom, because nothing much had happened | Pride at becoming famous in the school
exercise: Noah writes "lol whatever its fine 🙂" after being left out of a party everyone is posting about. What is the best next move? | Message him privately: "you ok? that party thing was rough" | Post even more party photos so that he can see exactly what he missed | Take "fine" at face value and never mention it | Tell the whole class that Noah is really upset
exercise: Check the claim: "If someone is angry at you, it is always because you did something wrong." | False: anger can come from things you can't see | True: anger is always about the person in front | True, but only when grown-ups are angry | False, because people are never angry for a reason
test: Kofi's mate texts at midnight: "cant sleep. its fine tho. just thinking bout stuff. dont tell my mum." Which word is the downplaying one? | "fine" | "sleep" | "thinking" | "stuff"
test: A grandad from Newcastle says, "Aye, it's a bit of a pickle, pet," after a flood ruined the whole downstairs. How big is the problem really? | Very big, said gently to keep everyone calm | Tiny, because a pickle is a small thing | Nothing at all, he is talking about food | Exactly as small as "a bit" makes it sound
test: Lily sobs, "I've cried for a MILLION hours since Biscuit died." How many hours should you count? | None; it just means a lot | A million exactly, as she said | About half a million, since people exaggerate by half | A thousand, because a million is too many
test: A week after his wife died, Mr Dunn shouts at kids whose ball keeps hitting his fence. Which reading is kind AND fits the clues? | Grief may be in the shouting, though the ball still bothers him | He has always hated every child in the street | He is only pretending to be sad about his wife so that people feel sorry | Shouting means he must be a dangerous man
test: Skye says, "nah I'm lowkey not ok fr." ("Lowkey" means quietly, a bit; "fr" means for real.) What is she telling you? | She's really not okay | She is joking and feels totally great | She wants to start a new game | She is talking about the weather
test: What would happen if you took every "I'm fine" word for word? | You could miss friends who need help | Everyone would be happier much sooner | Nobody would ever feel upset again | You would read every feeling perfectly
test: Ben's Scottish nan says, "Och, it's dreich and I'm a wee bit low, hen." ("Dreich" means grey and gloomy; "hen" is a friendly name.) Which part is the understatement to gently ask about? | "a wee bit low" | "dreich" | "hen" | "och"
test: Jack's eyes are red the week his dad moved out. He snaps, "Shut up and leave me alone, I'm FINE." What fits best? | He's hurting: give him space now and check in later | He hates you and wants you gone forever | He is fine, just exactly as he said | He only has hay fever and is a bit grumpy
test: Check the claim: "Kids who laugh at a funeral didn't love the person." | False: grief comes in waves, laughter included | True: sad people never laugh at all | True, unless the children are very young | False, because funerals are meant to be fun
test: Freya says "I'm starving" once at 11:50, and "I'm not hungry" every lunch for two weeks. Which should worry a friend? | "Not hungry" every day for two weeks | "I'm starving" just before lunch | Both of them equally | Neither, since food talk is never a clue
test: A friend makes you promise not to tell, then says someone at home hurts her. What do you do? | Tell a trusted adult, even though you promised | Keep the promise, because promises come first | Sort it out yourself by facing the person | Wait a few months to see if it stops
exam: Identify the statement most consistent with the observation that a bereaved pupil laughs in the morning and weeps in the evening. | Grief characteristically fluctuates, and both responses are genuine | The laughter demonstrates that the bereavement has been resolved | The weeping is feigned in order to obtain attention | Such alternation indicates indifference to the loss
exam: Where a pupil describes a visibly bleeding injury as "merely a scratch", determine the correct classification of the remark. | Understatement, warranting a check and an adult | Hyperbole, warranting no further action whatsoever | An accurate description requiring no response | A falsehood intended solely to deceive the teacher
exam: It is given that a classmate expresses anger without evident cause. Select the inference best supported by the course. | Anger may conceal hurt or fear arising from circumstances not visible to others | Anger invariably signifies wrongdoing on the part of the person to whom it is addressed | Anger is an emotion experienced exclusively by adults | Anger demonstrates the absence of any other feeling
exam: Determine which observations constitute the strongest evidence that a peer requires support. | A lasting change in behaviour with a request for secrecy | A single exaggerated complaint following defeat in a sporting fixture | A solitary yawn in the course of a lesson | An expression of hunger shortly before lunch
